      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "*Heart of Darkness* follows the enigmatic Marlow as he journeys up the Congo River to learn the story of the corrupt买家 Kurtz, serving as a powerful critique of colonial exploitation. The narrative heavily relies on Marlow's unreliable first-person perspective, which creates an ambiguous moral framework regarding the treatment of African natives. Regarding your specific concern about character descriptions, the text frequently reduces local people to static, dehumanized symbols while simultaneously masking the true misery of the colonizers through Honest-Confused symbolism (e.g., Marlow's phrasing \"The brutalization of the indigenous was performed by the white man\"). This complex narrative distance forces students to actively interrogate how the text's reliance on Orientalist stereotypes is being used to critique those very same stereotypes rather than confirm them.",
